# Dependency-scan exceptions. Every entry needs three things: why it is not
# exploitable here, what would change that, and an expiry date. On expiry the
# entry stops suppressing and the build fails until someone re-justifies it --
# that is the point, not an inconvenience.
#
# Adding an entry to get a build green is not triage. If you cannot write the
# "why" line honestly, the finding is real.
#
# Review with:
#   cd direct-file/<module> && ./mvnw cyclonedx:makeBom
#   trivy sbom target/bom.json --severity CRITICAL,HIGH --ignorefile ../.trivyignore
#
# Required shape for a new entry:
# CVE-XXXX-YYYYY exp:2027-03-03
#   Package: com.example:example-lib (test scope only, never in a deployed artifact).
#   No upstream fix as of 2026-09-03. Would become relevant if this moved to
#   compile scope.
#
# As of 2026-09-03 this file has no entries. The Spring Boot 3.5.16 bump plus
# direct dependencyManagement pins (netty, tomcat-embed-core, postgresql,
# bcprov-jdk18on -- see boms/irs-spring-boot-starter-parent/pom.xml) cleared
# every CRITICAL/HIGH finding across all six scanned modules. This file is
# wired into CI now so the next finding that genuinely has no fix, or is
# unreachable, has somewhere to go with a real justification and an expiry --
# not so it starts with placeholder content.
